foreign  [Music]  briefing for Thursday October 13th I'm  Zoe Thomas for The Wall Street Journal  if your job involves responding to  crises you might expect to work nights  weekends holidays and that's been the  situation for many cyber security  incident responders  as the number of hacks worldwide has  gone up so is their workload to a point  where according to a recent survey from  IBM over two-thirds of these workers  report being assigned two or more  incidents to respond to at once so what  does all this mean for an industry  that's crucial to protecting modern  businesses and how can companies protect  these overstretched workers joining us  to discuss that is wsj pro cyber  security reporter James Rundle hi James  hi sorry how you doing I'm doing great I  wonder if you can start by kind of  giving us a sense of the way work has  changed for the cyber security  professionals over you know just the  last few years yeah there's been a  significant change for every type of  cyber security professional but  particularly incident responders  obviously  cyber attacks have grown significantly  in the past few years both as a result  of the world becoming more networked  particularly during the pandemic but  also advances in technology on on the  part of the criminals as well so  really they're finding themselves  dealing with a massive influx of attacks  against companies of all stripes on all  sizes government agencies public bodies  and coming from all angles as well  criminals nation states and everything  in between from the people that you  spoke with I mean what kinds of symptoms  of overwork or stress did they describe  having to deal with what was their day  like overloaded I think in a word from a  lot of people I spoke to a lot of people  are working multiple jobs simultaneously  and that's a change from the past few  years as well you know previously this  was a job where you would go in person  to a company when it was attacked so  you'd be on site for days or even weeks  at a time so you can only focus on one  client with the pandemic that meant  everyone obviously was working from home  so for instance responders it meant they  could juggle more jobs at the same time  I spoke to one person who said that he  would sign on in a zoom call for one job  first thing in the morning move on to  the next one move on to the next one  then throughout the day just shift  between them as necessary it's been a  significant increase in their workload  and that comes with what you would  expect from that as well the symptoms of  burnout a lot of them described a lot of  them feel anxiety  some say that it's affecting their  personal lives IBM did some research  specifically around this area and they  said that around two-thirds of people  they surveyed said they'd sought some  kind of assistance for their mental  well-being recently just due to the  level of work and the stress that's  involved in that work as well what can  the companies do from their perspective  to help address this problem because it  doesn't sound like the incidence of of  cyber attacks are really going away  they're not and companies are aware of  this I have to say everyone I spoke to  in a senior role for the story was  acutely aware of the stress that their  employees were under because they've  done it themselves I mean this isn't a  career where you kind of go in at the  senior level without working in the  trenches so they've experienced it and  they understand that there are immense  pressures on their on their employees I  spoke to one fellow at salesforces so  that they give one Friday a month off  just for mental well-being for their  employees a lot of them have rules about  how many engagements their instant  responders can work on back to back at  IBM for instance no more than to and a  lot of them talked about just  communicating with the staff checking in  with them making sure that they are  taking care of themselves that they are  managing their stress properly and you  know some things are unavoidable in this  job cyber attacks generally happen on  holidays they happen on weekends at  inconvenient times and these guys are  expected to respond but for a lot of  them they just said it's a balancing act  and I think from the instant responders  perspective that IBM researcher  mentioned around well over three  quarters said that they felt supported  by their company which is a positive  sign I mean that that does at least  sound really positive I wonder though is  this something where the companies that  are being attacked should have been  putting in more cyber defenses so that  we don't see as many incidents needing  to be responded to I 100 uh and that was  a Common Thread from people I spoke to  about this work they just said they go  in to respond to these incidents and  often the work they have to do is  Complicated by the fact that the company  didn't have basic Protections in place  and this is a line you keep being  repeated by the Department of Homeland  Security by the Federal Bureau of  Investigation by anyone who really  responds to this saying that if you do  put basic Protections in place and you  make the effort now then it will  honestly prevents a lot of cyber attacks  and then if it doesn't then at least  reduce the time taken to recover and it  will also reduce the burden on your  instant responders and the cost to you  as well I mean a lot of them said that  at their engagements the first thing  they often have to do is an education  exercise to explain just what has been  going on to Executives who don't know  about cyber security  so let's talk about this problem then  moving forward is there a way apart from  beefing up their own cyber security that  companies can deal with this to help  prevent the burnout from happening in  the first place I think so what are the  main problems really is a lack of people  who are available to do these jobs the  the talent shortage in cyber security  has been pretty well documented by us  and others you know depending on what  statistics you look at there are over  800 000 unfilled positions in the US  right now over 2 million worldwide so a  lot of effort has to go into building  this pipeline of professionals who can  come in who can staff up these companies  so you're not just relying on a group of  people just to do you know increasing  amounts of work all the time and there  are efforts underway the White House  held a summit earlier this year on ways  in which the workforce can be developed  a lot of private companies are putting  initiatives in place to partner with  universities with schools and to train  internally to try and get these numbers  up and make sure that we have a  competitive stable and continuing  Workforce that we can grow all right  that was the wsg cyber security reporter  James Rundle thanks for breaking this  down for us James no problem  and that's it for today's Tech news  briefing for more Tech stories check out  our website wsj.com I'm Zoe Thomas for  The Wall Street Journal  thanks for listening  [Music]  [Music]

